nswer-06582eb3-c27f-5092-9e9b-b1e567c5c91d" "answer-488f0394-e755-5294-bf0e-26ae696c1638" "answer-05a416c3-5a8b-5bb4-9e2e-d5d5e3890c12"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-289f62f5-22f2-56ca-9282-386a0d800a7c" :answer/id #uuid "289f62f5-22f2-56ca-9282-386a0d800a7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "120\\,\\text{ft}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29a4bf25-d239-5564-8f8d-551b5e3bb452" :answer/id #uuid "29a4bf25-d239-5564-8f8d-551b5e3bb452" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "120\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06582eb3-c27f-5092-9e9b-b1e567c5c91d" :answer/id #uuid "06582eb3-c27f-5092-9e9b-b1e567c5c91d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "900\\,\\text{ft}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-488f0394-e755-5294-bf0e-26ae696c1638" :answer/id #uuid "488f0394-e755-5294-bf0e-26ae696c1638" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "900\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05a416c3-5a8b-5bb4-9e2e-d5d5e3890c12" :answer/id #uuid "05a416c3-5a8b-5bb4-9e2e-d5d5e3890c12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "900\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-44b69e39-7dc4-5902-a9e6-91ffdc7daa00" :question/id #uuid "44b69e39-7dc4-5902-a9e6-91ffdc7daa00" :question/math-academy-id "q-171696" :question/problem "The area of a square with a side length of $9\\,\\text{cm}$ is{{field-1}}\n${\\text{cm}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the area $A$ of a square, we square its side length. Therefore, the area of the square is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =(9\\,\\text{cm})^{2} \\\\  & =9\\,\\text{cm}\\times 9\\,\\text{cm} \\\\  & =(9\\times 9)\\times (\\text{cm}\\times \\text{cm}) \\\\  & =81\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-46046ba7-0cd7-533f-94c7-68f235237bf1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-46046ba7-0cd7-533f-94c7-68f235237bf1" :answer-field/id #uuid "46046ba7-0cd7-533f-94c7-68f235237bf1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9837b91d-0013-5137-84c2-410fdf7e1078" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9837b91d-0013-5137-84c2-410fdf7e1078"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9837b91d-0013-5137-84c2-410fdf7e1078" :answer/id #uuid "9837b91d-0013-5137-84c2-410fdf7e1078" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "81" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186189383 :tutorial/content "**Area** measures how much space there is on a flat surface. In this lesson, we'll learn about the various measurement units of area and how they relate to units of length. \n\nConsider a square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{m},$ shown below. \n\n![](images/3f/3feaac95102a8a3014b7ab114adb97d5a0267a5c654c584bff03152801c807c2.png)\n\nTo find the area of a square, we multiply its side lengths. Therefore, the area of this square is equal to\n\n$$\n1\\,\\textrm{m}\\times 1\\,\\textrm{m} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}^2.\n$$\n\nThe unit of area $(\\textrm m^2)$ we used is called a **square meter.** In this case, we say that the area of our square is **one square meter.**\n\nIn previous lessons, we learned that length can also be measured in kilometers $(\\textrm{km}),$ centimeters $(\\textrm{cm}),$ and millimeters $(\\textrm{mm}).$ Each of these length units has a corresponding area unit:\n\n- A square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{km}$ has an area of $1\\,\\textrm{km}^2,$ or **one square kilometer**.\n\n- A square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{cm}$ has an area of $1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2,$ or **one square centimeter**.\n\n- A square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{mm}$ has an area of $1\\,\\textrm{mm}^2,$ or **one square millimeter**.\n\nThe same idea applies when measuring area in customary units. For example:\n\n- A square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{in}$ has an area of $1\\,\\textrm{in}^2,$ or **one square inch**.\n\n- A square with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{ft}$ has an area of $1\\,\\textrm{ft}^2,$ or **one square foot**." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186189385 :tutorial/content "**Volume** measures how much three-dimensional space there is inside something. \n\nConsider a cube with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{m},$ shown below. \n\n![](images/f0/f0006ee19c09fcf56ad032675f5f71ca8e49fc4c554a169e17bff0a09f2bbb6a.png)\n\nTo find the volume of a cube, we multiply its side lengths. Therefore, the volume of this cube is equal to\n\n$$\n1\\,\\textrm{m}\\times 1\\,\\textrm{m}\\times 1\\,\\textrm{m} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}^3.\n$$\n\nThe unit of volume $(\\textrm m^3)$ we used is called a **cubic meter.** In this case, we say that the volume of our cube is **one cubic meter.**\n\nAs with area, each unit of length has a corresponding volume unit. For example:\n\n- A cube with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{cm}$ has a volume of $1\\,\\textrm{cm}^3,$ or **one cubic centimeter**.\n\n- A cube with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{in}$ has a volume of $1\\,\\textrm{in}^3,$ or **one cubic inch**." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186189389 :tutorial/math-academy-id 15346 :tutorial/content "A square is a special type of rectangle whose length and width are equal. For example, consider the square below.\n\n![](images/a9/a92233299ba67fec2a7712c88afcabeff3973dda07afdd7f91c085c4564e2aa1.png)\n\nWe say that the **side length** of this square equals $3\\,\\textrm{m}.$\n\nSince a square is a special type of rectangle, we find its area by multiplying its length and width. However, since its length and width are equal, we can find its area by *squaring* its side length:\n\nTherefore, the area of the square is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =(3\\,\\text{m})^{2}\\text{ } \\\\  & =3\\,\\text{m}\\times 3\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =(3\\times 3)\\times (\\text{m}\\times \\text{m}) \\\\  & =9\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186189433 :tutorial/content "A **cube** is a special type of rectangular solid whose length, width, and height are equal. \n\n![](images/e6/e696b4b9875052f18f394df4f0c6fb867b599a8484ac04acfca021ade9618a49.png)\n\nWe say that the **side length** of this cube equals $2\\,\\textrm{cm}.$\n\nSince a cube is a special type of rectangular solid, we find its volume by multiplying its length, width, and height. However, since its length, width, and height are all equal, we can find its volume by *cubing* its side length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =(2\\,\\text{cm})^{3} \\\\  & =(2\\,\\text{cm})\\times (2\\,\\text{cm})\\times (2\\,\\text{cm}) \\\\  & =(2\\times 2\\times 2)\\times (\\text{cm}\\times \\text{cm}\\times \\text{cm}) \\\\  & =8\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188466 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186189141 :step/math-academy-id 633751 :db/ensure [:step/validate]}]
